1
00:00:00,862 --> 00:00:02,762
In the first three episodes we turned a

2
00:00:02,762 --> 00:00:04,532
batch of papers into a wiki.

3
00:00:05,274 --> 00:00:07,532
This episode uses it the other way round.

4
00:00:08,274 --> 00:00:10,174
A knowledge base is not only something you

5
00:00:10,174 --> 00:00:13,357
look things up in. It can also teach you.

6
00:00:14,099 --> 00:00:16,512
We take the first topic page, FlyCircuit

7
00:00:16,512 --> 00:00:18,562
database, and show how one page

8
00:00:18,562 --> 00:00:19,844
turns into a course.

9
00:00:21,209 --> 00:00:24,372
Why a topic page? Because it already looks

10
00:00:24,372 --> 00:00:27,479
like a syllabus. Seven sections.

11
00:00:28,222 --> 00:00:30,567
Every number linked back to a paper entry.

12
00:00:31,309 --> 00:00:33,529
And a list of open questions at the end.

13
00:00:34,272 --> 00:00:35,742
So the approach is direct.

14
00:00:36,484 --> 00:00:38,904
Seven sections become seven lessons.

15
00:00:39,647 --> 00:00:41,297
The entries become the numbers and the

16
00:00:41,297 --> 00:00:42,654
sources in the lecture.

17
00:00:43,397 --> 00:00:45,409
The open questions become things to think

18
00:00:45,409 --> 00:00:48,554
about. The AI lays the structure out,

19
00:00:48,659 --> 00:00:50,384
links the numbers back to where they came

20
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:52,697
from, and rewrites the text in a teaching

21
00:00:52,697 --> 00:00:56,347
voice. What to teach, how deep to go, and

22
00:00:56,347 --> 00:00:58,184
which line is the point, that is still

23
00:00:58,184 --> 00:01:00,647
yours to decide. Now here is

24
00:01:00,647 --> 00:01:01,779
the course itself.

25
00:01:03,157 --> 00:01:06,202
Lesson one. What FlyCircuit is.

26
00:01:06,944 --> 00:01:09,044
It is a public image library of neurons

27
00:01:09,044 --> 00:01:10,402
from the fruit fly brain.

28
00:01:11,144 --> 00:01:12,982
Each record in it is the complete three

29
00:01:12,982 --> 00:01:14,839
dimensional image of one neuron.

30
00:01:15,582 --> 00:01:17,782
The method is to make only one neuron in a

31
00:01:17,782 --> 00:01:20,219
fly brain glow, image its shape in three

32
00:01:20,219 --> 00:01:22,644
dimensions, then register that image into

33
00:01:22,644 --> 00:01:24,852
the coordinate system of a standard brain.

34
00:01:25,594 --> 00:01:27,819
Repeat that tens of thousands of times and

35
00:01:27,819 --> 00:01:29,519
you have tens of thousands of neurons

36
00:01:29,519 --> 00:01:31,402
sitting in one coordinate system.

37
00:01:32,144 --> 00:01:35,627
Notice the unit. One record is one neuron.

38
00:01:36,369 --> 00:01:39,014
Not one fly, and not one photograph.

39
00:01:39,757 --> 00:01:42,064
Every number after this counts neurons.

40
00:01:43,448 --> 00:01:45,261
On screen is the first figure of the

41
00:01:45,261 --> 00:01:48,448
twenty eleven paper. Column A, from the

42
00:01:48,448 --> 00:01:51,523
top. One sample brain with three glowing

43
00:01:51,523 --> 00:01:54,656
neurons. They are cut out one at a time,

44
00:01:54,748 --> 00:01:56,498
and then put back into the brain they came

45
00:01:56,498 --> 00:01:59,798
from. On the right, panel C shows seven

46
00:01:59,798 --> 00:02:02,123
neurons from seven different fly lines and

47
00:02:02,123 --> 00:02:04,473
seven different flies, all placed in one

48
00:02:04,473 --> 00:02:06,798
standard brain. That is what the

49
00:02:06,798 --> 00:02:08,306
database is built to do.

50
00:02:09,676 --> 00:02:12,521
Lesson two. How it was built.

51
00:02:13,263 --> 00:02:16,426
Three techniques. First, light up one

52
00:02:16,426 --> 00:02:19,451
neuron at a time. Genetic tools make a

53
00:02:19,451 --> 00:02:21,401
fluorescent protein appear in only a

54
00:02:21,401 --> 00:02:23,551
handful of neurons, and everything else

55
00:02:23,551 --> 00:02:26,621
stays dark. The method is not hard.

56
00:02:27,363 --> 00:02:30,909
The scale is. Second, a standard brain.

57
00:02:31,651 --> 00:02:33,513
Tens of thousands of neurons come from

58
00:02:33,513 --> 00:02:35,689
tens of thousands of flies, and every

59
00:02:35,689 --> 00:02:37,383
brain is slightly different.

60
00:02:38,126 --> 00:02:39,963
To put them all in one coordinate system

61
00:02:39,963 --> 00:02:41,534
you need a base map first.

62
00:02:42,276 --> 00:02:45,438
Third, registration. A mathematical

63
00:02:45,438 --> 00:02:47,651
transform pastes each neuron image onto

64
00:02:47,651 --> 00:02:48,883
that standard brain.

65
00:02:50,256 --> 00:02:52,118
There is a decision inside the standard

66
00:02:52,118 --> 00:02:54,051
brain step that is worth remembering.

67
00:02:54,793 --> 00:02:56,843
The team did not average dozens of brains

68
00:02:56,843 --> 00:02:59,743
together to make it. Averaging blurs the

69
00:02:59,743 --> 00:03:01,831
image, and the boundaries between brain

70
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:04,693
regions disappear. Instead they used

71
00:03:04,693 --> 00:03:06,781
thirty four samples to compute the average

72
00:03:06,781 --> 00:03:09,031
shape, and then picked the one real sample

73
00:03:09,031 --> 00:03:11,026
that came closest to that average.

74
00:03:11,768 --> 00:03:13,663
The upside is sharp boundaries.

75
00:03:14,406 --> 00:03:16,218
The cost is that the standard brain is

76
00:03:16,218 --> 00:03:18,006
still one individual, and the whole

77
00:03:18,006 --> 00:03:19,356
coordinate system is tied

78
00:03:19,356 --> 00:03:20,526
to that individual.

79
00:03:21,907 --> 00:03:23,952
This is how the standard brain was made.

80
00:03:24,695 --> 00:03:26,582
Panels B through F compute the average

81
00:03:26,582 --> 00:03:28,507
inner and outer surface from thirty four

82
00:03:28,507 --> 00:03:31,777
samples. Panel G is the key step.

83
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:34,357
The average is used to pick the closest

84
00:03:34,357 --> 00:03:36,527
real brain, not used as the brain.

85
00:03:37,270 --> 00:03:38,720
Panel H then cuts it into

86
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,152
fifty eight neuropils.

87
00:03:41,535 --> 00:03:43,647
Registration has error, and the paper

88
00:03:43,647 --> 00:03:46,585
measured it. They used one kind of neuron

89
00:03:46,585 --> 00:03:48,560
that every fly has and that follows a

90
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:51,622
fixed path. With whole brain registration

91
00:03:51,622 --> 00:03:53,685
alone, the terminals land in the right

92
00:03:53,685 --> 00:03:55,780
place sixty nine percent of the time.

93
00:03:56,522 --> 00:03:58,785
Add one more round of local registration

94
00:03:58,785 --> 00:04:00,705
and it reaches ninety five percent.

95
00:04:01,447 --> 00:04:03,030
Remember those two numbers.

96
00:04:03,772 --> 00:04:05,892
They are not a score for this one paper.

97
00:04:06,635 --> 00:04:08,510
They are the accuracy ceiling for every

98
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:10,647
cross individual analysis built on this

99
00:04:10,647 --> 00:04:13,747
whole dataset. Analysis at the level of

100
00:04:13,747 --> 00:04:15,105
brain regions holds up.

101
00:04:15,847 --> 00:04:17,985
Claims about position finer than a region

102
00:04:17,985 --> 00:04:19,455
are outside the guarantee.

103
00:04:20,842 --> 00:04:22,542
This figure is where those two numbers

104
00:04:22,542 --> 00:04:25,580
come from. Panel B shows the same neuron

105
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:27,037
in ten different flies.

106
00:04:27,780 --> 00:04:29,705
On the left of panel C is whole brain

107
00:04:29,705 --> 00:04:32,405
registration. On the right is local

108
00:04:32,405 --> 00:04:34,692
registration, and the fibers pull in much

109
00:04:34,692 --> 00:04:37,730
tighter. The two bars in panel D are the

110
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:39,725
sixty nine and the ninety five.

111
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:42,830
In panel F, that spray of points is where

112
00:04:42,830 --> 00:04:45,105
ten samples land around the matching point

113
00:04:45,105 --> 00:04:46,325
on the standard brain.

114
00:04:47,067 --> 00:04:48,837
The spread is the error itself.

115
00:04:50,214 --> 00:04:51,946
Here is something that gets forgotten.

116
00:04:52,689 --> 00:04:54,526
Neurons are labeled with genetic driver

117
00:04:54,526 --> 00:04:57,289
lines. Which neurons made it into the

118
00:04:57,289 --> 00:04:59,289
database is decided by the drivers you

119
00:04:59,289 --> 00:05:02,314
use. It is not a uniform sample of the

120
00:05:02,314 --> 00:05:05,251
whole brain. The twenty eleven paper did

121
00:05:05,251 --> 00:05:06,709
the arithmetic itself.

122
00:05:07,451 --> 00:05:09,701
Nine driver lines together label more than

123
00:05:09,701 --> 00:05:11,671
one hundred fifty thousand neurons.

124
00:05:12,414 --> 00:05:14,064
That is fifty percent more than the

125
00:05:14,064 --> 00:05:16,451
estimate for the entire brain, which means

126
00:05:16,451 --> 00:05:18,564
those drivers do not faithfully reflect

127
00:05:18,564 --> 00:05:20,246
what is really expressed in there.

128
00:05:20,989 --> 00:05:23,214
This bias travels with the data into every

129
00:05:23,214 --> 00:05:24,609
analysis downstream.

130
00:05:25,994 --> 00:05:28,851
Lesson three. Three versions.

131
00:05:29,594 --> 00:05:31,656
Version one point zero, from twenty

132
00:05:31,656 --> 00:05:34,189
eleven, about sixteen thousand neurons.

133
00:05:34,931 --> 00:05:36,869
Version one point one, from twenty

134
00:05:36,869 --> 00:05:38,956
fifteen, twenty three thousand five

135
00:05:38,956 --> 00:05:40,239
hundred seventy nine.

136
00:05:40,981 --> 00:05:42,981
Version one point two, from twenty

137
00:05:42,981 --> 00:05:45,044
nineteen, twenty eight thousand five

138
00:05:45,044 --> 00:05:46,326
hundred seventy three.

139
00:05:47,069 --> 00:05:48,769
What separates the versions is more than

140
00:05:48,769 --> 00:05:51,769
the count. Version one point one added

141
00:05:51,769 --> 00:05:54,594
something crucial. Every neuron got an

142
00:05:54,594 --> 00:05:56,806
annotation for which end takes signals in

143
00:05:56,806 --> 00:05:58,314
and which end sends them out.

144
00:05:59,056 --> 00:06:00,819
Only with that can you build a network

145
00:06:00,819 --> 00:06:02,076
that has direction in it.

146
00:06:02,819 --> 00:06:04,806
The information flow analysis in the

147
00:06:04,806 --> 00:06:07,056
twenty fifteen paper rests on exactly

148
00:06:07,056 --> 00:06:10,006
this. So when you cite any FlyCircuit

149
00:06:10,006 --> 00:06:11,676
number, say which version.

150
00:06:13,053 --> 00:06:14,736
Is twenty eight thousand enough?

151
00:06:15,478 --> 00:06:17,516
The whole fruit fly brain holds somewhere

152
00:06:17,516 --> 00:06:19,266
between one hundred thousand and one

153
00:06:19,266 --> 00:06:21,516
hundred thirty five thousand neurons, and

154
00:06:21,516 --> 00:06:23,748
the estimates differ from paper to paper.

155
00:06:24,491 --> 00:06:26,591
So version one point two is about one

156
00:06:26,591 --> 00:06:29,466
fifth of it. The twenty eleven paper

157
00:06:29,466 --> 00:06:30,961
answered the question this way.

158
00:06:31,703 --> 00:06:33,753
Take a random slice of the data and see

159
00:06:33,753 --> 00:06:35,491
whether it produces the same connection

160
00:06:35,491 --> 00:06:37,061
pattern as the whole set.

161
00:06:37,803 --> 00:06:39,941
One tenth was enough to reach zero point

162
00:06:39,941 --> 00:06:41,248
nine seven agreement.

163
00:06:41,991 --> 00:06:44,136
But be careful about what that proves.

164
00:06:44,878 --> 00:06:46,928
It compares a subset of the data against

165
00:06:46,928 --> 00:06:49,853
the full set. It proves the dataset is

166
00:06:49,853 --> 00:06:51,311
internally consistent.

167
00:06:52,053 --> 00:06:53,778
It does not prove that the four fifths

168
00:06:53,778 --> 00:06:55,511
never sampled look like this.

169
00:06:56,253 --> 00:06:57,898
Those are two different things.

170
00:06:59,273 --> 00:07:01,385
Panel F is the evidence for that one tenth

171
00:07:01,385 --> 00:07:04,048
claim. The filled circles are the real

172
00:07:04,048 --> 00:07:07,060
data. By the time the sample reaches one

173
00:07:07,060 --> 00:07:09,535
tenth, the correlation is already close to

174
00:07:09,535 --> 00:07:12,310
one. The open circles are a random

175
00:07:12,310 --> 00:07:14,523
network, which needs ninety five percent

176
00:07:14,523 --> 00:07:17,755
to catch up. Panel G explains why.

177
00:07:18,498 --> 00:07:20,255
Connections follow a power law.

178
00:07:20,998 --> 00:07:22,873
A few nodes carry a huge share of the

179
00:07:22,873 --> 00:07:24,848
links, so a sparse sample still

180
00:07:24,848 --> 00:07:26,155
picks up the skeleton.

181
00:07:27,540 --> 00:07:30,703
Lesson four. What each of the six papers

182
00:07:30,703 --> 00:07:33,335
takes from it. Three kinds.

183
00:07:34,078 --> 00:07:36,298
Building it, the twenty eleven paper.

184
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:39,548
Using it for analysis, two papers.

185
00:07:40,290 --> 00:07:42,665
Twenty fifteen takes version one point one

186
00:07:42,665 --> 00:07:44,590
and builds a directed network at the level

187
00:07:44,590 --> 00:07:47,628
of brain regions. Twenty nineteen takes

188
00:07:47,628 --> 00:07:49,653
version one point two and builds one at

189
00:07:49,653 --> 00:07:51,335
the level of single neurons.

190
00:07:52,078 --> 00:07:54,398
The same tools, run at two scales.

191
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:57,360
Building tools for it, three papers.

192
00:07:58,103 --> 00:08:00,153
Twenty eighteen solves how to look at tens

193
00:08:00,153 --> 00:08:01,973
of thousands of neurons at once.

194
00:08:02,715 --> 00:08:04,853
Twenty twenty one automates segmentation

195
00:08:04,853 --> 00:08:06,485
that used to be done by hand.

196
00:08:07,228 --> 00:08:09,315
Twenty twenty four segments brain regions

197
00:08:09,315 --> 00:08:12,290
automatically. What the three tool papers

198
00:08:12,290 --> 00:08:14,053
share is that they validate on the whole

199
00:08:14,053 --> 00:08:16,935
database, not on a few good looking cases.

200
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:20,527
One figure for each of the three uses.

201
00:08:21,270 --> 00:08:23,495
On the left is the LPU map from twenty

202
00:08:23,495 --> 00:08:25,782
eleven, which cuts the brain into blocks

203
00:08:25,782 --> 00:08:28,040
that each carry their own local circuitry.

204
00:08:28,782 --> 00:08:30,820
In the middle is the neuron level network

205
00:08:30,820 --> 00:08:32,882
from twenty nineteen, nearly twenty

206
00:08:32,882 --> 00:08:34,470
thousand points sorted into eight

207
00:08:34,470 --> 00:08:37,590
communities. On the right is Kaleido.

208
00:08:38,332 --> 00:08:40,145
Ten thousand neurons shown at the same

209
00:08:40,145 --> 00:08:42,440
time, and you can still tell them apart.

210
00:08:43,828 --> 00:08:45,935
There is an easy confusion hiding here.

211
00:08:46,678 --> 00:08:49,003
Raw brain images and single neuron images

212
00:08:49,003 --> 00:08:50,385
are two different numbers.

213
00:08:51,128 --> 00:08:53,178
One brain image can contain more than one

214
00:08:53,178 --> 00:08:55,953
neuron. The twenty twenty one paper

215
00:08:55,953 --> 00:08:57,803
started from twenty two thousand thirty

216
00:08:57,803 --> 00:08:59,940
seven raw images and retrieved twenty

217
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:01,665
eight thousand one hundred twenty five

218
00:09:01,665 --> 00:09:04,765
neurons. That is a ratio of about one

219
00:09:04,765 --> 00:09:07,615
point two eight. So when you say how many

220
00:09:07,615 --> 00:09:10,040
records FlyCircuit has, be clear about

221
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,653
whether you are counting brains or

222
00:09:11,653 --> 00:09:12,823
counting neurons.

223
00:09:14,207 --> 00:09:16,327
Lesson five is the heart of this course.

224
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:18,527
It is reading practice.

225
00:09:19,270 --> 00:09:21,207
Three places where the numbers do not line

226
00:09:21,207 --> 00:09:25,295
up. First. The twenty eleven paper says

227
00:09:25,295 --> 00:09:27,640
forty one LPUs plus six hubs.

228
00:09:28,382 --> 00:09:30,670
When the twenty fifteen paper cites it, it

229
00:09:30,670 --> 00:09:32,465
says forty three plus six.

230
00:09:33,207 --> 00:09:34,840
Both add up to forty nine.

231
00:09:35,582 --> 00:09:37,465
The classification is different.

232
00:09:38,207 --> 00:09:40,407
Which two units changed category is not

233
00:09:40,407 --> 00:09:41,890
explained in either paper.

234
00:09:42,632 --> 00:09:45,707
Second. That number twenty eight thousand

235
00:09:45,707 --> 00:09:47,277
five hundred seventy three.

236
00:09:48,020 --> 00:09:50,170
Twenty nineteen and twenty twenty one both

237
00:09:50,170 --> 00:09:53,282
call it neurons. Twenty twenty four calls

238
00:09:53,282 --> 00:09:56,220
it brains. Going by the twenty twenty one

239
00:09:56,220 --> 00:09:58,470
data, brain images should be around twenty

240
00:09:58,470 --> 00:10:00,595
two thousand, so the sentence in twenty

241
00:10:00,595 --> 00:10:02,640
twenty four is probably a slip.

242
00:10:03,382 --> 00:10:06,470
Third. Automatic segmentation returned

243
00:10:06,470 --> 00:10:08,295
twenty eight thousand one hundred twenty

244
00:10:08,295 --> 00:10:11,420
five neurons. That is four hundred forty

245
00:10:11,420 --> 00:10:13,377
eight fewer than the manual library.

246
00:10:14,120 --> 00:10:15,757
Where those four hundred went, the

247
00:10:15,757 --> 00:10:17,165
text does not say.

248
00:10:18,547 --> 00:10:20,434
Not one of those three is an arithmetic

249
00:10:20,434 --> 00:10:22,959
mistake. All three are different

250
00:10:22,959 --> 00:10:24,429
measurement conventions.

251
00:10:25,172 --> 00:10:27,197
A different classification, a different

252
00:10:27,197 --> 00:10:29,304
unit, a different moment of sampling.

253
00:10:30,047 --> 00:10:32,022
You cannot see any of it in one paper on

254
00:10:32,022 --> 00:10:34,984
its own. You see it when six papers are

255
00:10:34,984 --> 00:10:36,479
lined up side by side.

256
00:10:37,222 --> 00:10:39,654
That is the reason a topic page exists.

257
00:10:40,397 --> 00:10:41,947
If you remember one thing from this

258
00:10:41,947 --> 00:10:43,567
course, remember this.

259
00:10:44,309 --> 00:10:46,384
When you cite a count, state the version

260
00:10:46,384 --> 00:10:48,972
and state the unit. That reflex

261
00:10:48,972 --> 00:10:50,254
works in any field.

262
00:10:51,638 --> 00:10:54,846
Lesson six. The limits of the data.

263
00:10:55,588 --> 00:10:57,471
Three of them, and they are built in.

264
00:10:58,213 --> 00:11:00,571
They are not defects in any one paper.

265
00:11:01,313 --> 00:11:03,188
They are properties of the optical single

266
00:11:03,188 --> 00:11:04,546
neuron imaging route.

267
00:11:05,288 --> 00:11:07,508
First, you cannot see synapses.

268
00:11:08,251 --> 00:11:09,838
So connection here means something

269
00:11:09,838 --> 00:11:13,038
specific. Two neurons have fibers running

270
00:11:13,038 --> 00:11:15,188
into the same region, so we presume they

271
00:11:15,188 --> 00:11:16,508
have a chance of touching.

272
00:11:17,251 --> 00:11:19,113
The twenty nineteen paper checked this

273
00:11:19,113 --> 00:11:20,851
against a set of neurons that had been

274
00:11:20,851 --> 00:11:22,733
carefully dissected and described.

275
00:11:23,476 --> 00:11:25,551
Of the pairs that really are connected, it

276
00:11:25,551 --> 00:11:27,371
catches only forty percent.

277
00:11:28,113 --> 00:11:30,183
Second, direction is predicted.

278
00:11:30,926 --> 00:11:32,951
Which end receives and which end sends is

279
00:11:32,951 --> 00:11:34,838
guessed from the shape of the neuron, and

280
00:11:34,838 --> 00:11:36,613
nobody has measured how accurate that

281
00:11:36,613 --> 00:11:40,183
guess is. Third, sampling is biased.

282
00:11:40,926 --> 00:11:42,983
That is the driver line problem again.

283
00:11:44,362 --> 00:11:46,462
Put the three together and what FlyCircuit

284
00:11:46,462 --> 00:11:48,187
gives you is a connection map that is

285
00:11:48,187 --> 00:11:50,012
mesoscale, directed but with the

286
00:11:50,012 --> 00:11:52,199
directions predicted, and biased in what

287
00:11:52,199 --> 00:11:55,199
got sampled. Inside that description it

288
00:11:55,199 --> 00:11:56,619
does its job very well.

289
00:11:57,362 --> 00:11:59,807
Any claim that reaches past it needs care.

290
00:12:00,549 --> 00:12:02,249
The order of the three limits is worth

291
00:12:02,249 --> 00:12:05,562
noticing too. No synapses decides what the

292
00:12:05,562 --> 00:12:06,932
word connection means.

293
00:12:07,674 --> 00:12:09,649
Predicted direction decides how far you

294
00:12:09,649 --> 00:12:11,157
can trust the word directed.

295
00:12:11,899 --> 00:12:13,824
Biased sampling decides how well whole

296
00:12:13,824 --> 00:12:15,882
brain really stands for the whole brain.

297
00:12:16,624 --> 00:12:18,799
Keep those three sentences beside you as

298
00:12:18,799 --> 00:12:20,737
you read the papers that follow, and a

299
00:12:20,737 --> 00:12:22,649
quiet under these assumptions appears in

300
00:12:22,649 --> 00:12:24,107
front of every conclusion.

301
00:12:25,494 --> 00:12:28,676
Lesson seven. Where it sits on the map.

302
00:12:29,419 --> 00:12:31,576
FlyCircuit cannot see synapses.

303
00:12:32,319 --> 00:12:35,344
So what can? Electron microscopy

304
00:12:35,344 --> 00:12:38,281
connectomes. Around twenty twenty they

305
00:12:38,281 --> 00:12:40,656
supplied synapse level resolution and real

306
00:12:40,656 --> 00:12:43,701
direction. The two are complementary.

307
00:12:44,444 --> 00:12:46,639
Optics gives you the whole brain skeleton.

308
00:12:47,381 --> 00:12:49,519
Electron microscopy gives you precise

309
00:12:49,519 --> 00:12:52,481
local wiring. The knowledge base already

310
00:12:52,481 --> 00:12:54,531
holds one analysis built on electron

311
00:12:54,531 --> 00:12:57,306
microscopy data. And there is a third

312
00:12:57,306 --> 00:13:00,694
route. Expansion microscopy, from twenty

313
00:13:00,694 --> 00:13:03,481
twenty five. You swell the sample up

314
00:13:03,481 --> 00:13:05,519
physically so that light microscopy can

315
00:13:05,519 --> 00:13:07,444
reach the dense reconstruction electron

316
00:13:07,444 --> 00:13:10,881
microscopy achieves. It is optical, like

317
00:13:10,881 --> 00:13:12,831
FlyCircuit, but the strategy is the

318
00:13:12,831 --> 00:13:15,606
opposite. One of them sidesteps the

319
00:13:15,606 --> 00:13:17,826
resolution problem with sparse labeling.

320
00:13:18,569 --> 00:13:20,344
The other solves it head on by making

321
00:13:20,344 --> 00:13:21,376
the tissue bigger.

322
00:13:22,753 --> 00:13:24,711
On the left is the LICONN paper.

323
00:13:25,453 --> 00:13:28,098
A small block of mouse cortex, expanded,

324
00:13:28,153 --> 00:13:30,078
then densely reconstructed with a light

325
00:13:30,078 --> 00:13:32,141
microscope, right down to labeling the

326
00:13:32,141 --> 00:13:34,511
molecules on either side of a synapse.

327
00:13:35,253 --> 00:13:37,291
On the right is a connection matrix built

328
00:13:37,291 --> 00:13:39,803
from electron microscopy data, projection

329
00:13:39,803 --> 00:13:41,461
neurons to Kenyon cells.

330
00:13:42,203 --> 00:13:43,953
That L shape is the finding of the whole

331
00:13:43,953 --> 00:13:46,828
paper. What the two figures share is that

332
00:13:46,828 --> 00:13:48,598
they see what FlyCircuit cannot.

333
00:13:49,341 --> 00:13:50,778
The cost is that they only look

334
00:13:50,778 --> 00:13:51,998
at one small block.

335
00:13:53,373 --> 00:13:55,705
So FlyCircuit has not been replaced.

336
00:13:56,448 --> 00:13:59,323
It has been placed. Knowing where it sits

337
00:13:59,323 --> 00:14:00,835
on the map is what tells you which

338
00:14:00,835 --> 00:14:02,673
questions to bring here and which ones

339
00:14:02,673 --> 00:14:04,605
belong on another topic page.

340
00:14:05,348 --> 00:14:07,573
The topic page already ends with a list of

341
00:14:07,573 --> 00:14:10,585
related topics. By the time the course

342
00:14:10,585 --> 00:14:12,610
reaches this point, the next course is

343
00:14:12,610 --> 00:14:13,730
already scheduled.

344
00:14:15,112 --> 00:14:17,132
Seven lessons on one database.

345
00:14:17,875 --> 00:14:19,945
It comes down to three reflexes.

346
00:14:20,687 --> 00:14:22,675
First, state the version and state the

347
00:14:22,675 --> 00:14:26,150
unit. Second, there is an invisible word,

348
00:14:26,150 --> 00:14:28,395
presumed, in front of every connection.

349
00:14:29,137 --> 00:14:31,170
A positive statement is trustworthy.

350
00:14:31,912 --> 00:14:33,795
A negative one needs a discount.

351
00:14:34,537 --> 00:14:36,612
Third, three premises follow you all the

352
00:14:36,612 --> 00:14:39,625
way to the end. Registration error, a

353
00:14:39,625 --> 00:14:41,287
standard brain that is really one

354
00:14:41,287 --> 00:14:43,725
individual, and driver lines deciding who

355
00:14:43,725 --> 00:14:46,650
gets in. Later papers stop mentioning

356
00:14:46,650 --> 00:14:49,545
them. That does not mean they went away.

357
00:14:50,916 --> 00:14:53,336
Last, back to the theme of this episode.

358
00:14:54,078 --> 00:14:55,378
How did this course come out of the

359
00:14:55,378 --> 00:14:58,616
knowledge base? The seven lessons map one

360
00:14:58,616 --> 00:15:00,378
to one onto the seven sections of the

361
00:15:00,378 --> 00:15:03,466
topic page. The numbers and the sources

362
00:15:03,466 --> 00:15:05,203
were pulled back from the paper entries

363
00:15:05,203 --> 00:15:07,791
and checked. The things to think about

364
00:15:07,791 --> 00:15:09,523
came from the open questions.

365
00:15:10,266 --> 00:15:11,941
The quiz came from those three numbers

366
00:15:11,941 --> 00:15:14,628
that do not line up. The AI laid the

367
00:15:14,628 --> 00:15:16,778
structure out, linked everything back to a

368
00:15:16,778 --> 00:15:18,798
source, and rewrote the voice.

369
00:15:19,541 --> 00:15:20,948
You decided the rest.

370
00:15:21,691 --> 00:15:23,698
That a topic page is a syllabus.

371
00:15:24,441 --> 00:15:26,341
That registration error is the number to

372
00:15:26,341 --> 00:15:27,786
remember in lesson two.

373
00:15:28,528 --> 00:15:30,416
That lesson five is the point of the whole

374
00:15:30,416 --> 00:15:33,873
course. Same as the first three episodes.

375
00:15:34,616 --> 00:15:36,591
The structural decisions happen before the

376
00:15:36,591 --> 00:15:37,961
AI starts working.

377
00:15:39,344 --> 00:15:41,056
There are eight more topic pages in the

378
00:15:41,056 --> 00:15:44,131
knowledge base. The same method can run

379
00:15:44,131 --> 00:15:47,256
eight more times. Which one comes next has

380
00:15:47,256 --> 00:15:48,639
not been decided yet.

381
00:15:49,381 --> 00:15:50,639
See you next time.
